# TIL — 2026-04-21
TypeScript's `satisfies` operator (since 4.9) lets you assert that a value conforms to a type without widening its inferred literal types.

```ts
type Config = {
  mode: "development" | "production";
  port: number;
  debug?: boolean;
};

// Using a type annotation: literals widen to the declared type
const cfgA: Config = { mode: "production", port: 8080 };
// typeof cfgA.mode === string  (loses literal "production")

// Using `satisfies`: keeps literal inference, still checked against Config
const cfgB = { mode: "production", port: 8080 } satisfies Config;
// typeof cfgB.mode === "production" (literal preserved)

// `satisfies` still enforces shape:
const bad = { mode: "prod", port: "8080" } satisfies Config; // Error: "prod" not allowed, port must be number
```

Use `satisfies` when you want strict shape checking but also preserve literal types for downstream narrowing or exhaustive checks.